        <!--
        ANALYSIS-UNIT CHECKLIST
        1. Write one sentence that uniquely identifies the primary row of analysis.
        2. Decide how repeated attempts by the same student on the same item are handled.
        3. Decide whether unsubmitted attempts contribute to process descriptions.
        4. Decide whether attempts continuing after the first correct check are truncated.
        5. Define feature-specific denominators for no-check, one-check, and multi-check attempts.
        6. Generate a CONSORT-style data-flow table even if no diagram is used.
        7. Verify that exclusions do not depend on desirable or undesirable outcomes.
        -->
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-validation-analysis">
        <title>Validation and Sensitivity Analyses</title>

        <p>
          RQ2 is evaluated with exact anchor agreement, anchor coverage, readiness
          proportions, item-specific variation, mismatch-distance summaries,
          manual audit, synthetic fault injection, and golden regression fixtures.
          Proportions are reported with uncertainty intervals, and item-level
          results are shown to identify concentrated failures that an aggregate
          rate could conceal.
        </p>

        <p>
          RQ4 compares the primary configuration with prespecified alternatives
          for attempt-gap threshold, reset boundaries, treatment of events after
          correctness, no-op inclusion, row-order tie handling, analytic idle
          threshold, and inclusion of limited attempts.  Comparisons report changes
          in sample size, anchor agreement, and key feature summaries.
        </p>

        <!--
        SENSITIVITY-ANALYSIS CHECKLIST
        1. Create a configuration table containing the primary value and every alternative.
        2. Justify alternatives using platform semantics or observed gap distributions,
           not by which setting produces preferred results.
        3. Re-run the complete reconstruction and feature pipeline for each configuration.
        4. Compare attempts, ready percentage, anchor agreement, median effective moves,
           median active time, checking frequency, and the primary RQ3 contrast.
        5. Report absolute differences and percentage changes with uncertainty where useful.
        6. Identify conclusions that are stable, qualified, or reversed.
        7. Archive every configuration and output under a stable run identifier.
        -->
      </subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="subsec-process-analysis">
        <title>Analysis of Process Information Beyond Correctness</title>
        
        <warning>
        <p>
          [STUB: Select one primary endpoint-equivalence comparison.  A strong
          option is to compare attempts that are eventually correct but differ in
          first-check correctness, while also distinguishing direct correct
          attempts from revised correct attempts.  Summarize movement, checking,
          error profile, and active time without implying a unique cognitive
          interpretation.]
        </p>

        <p>
          [STUB: Specify the primary descriptive estimands and, only if warranted,
          one crossed student-item model.  With a limited number of items, item
          effects should primarily control dependence rather than support broad
          item-design generalization.]
        </p>
        </warning>

        <!--
        RQ3 ANALYSIS CHECKLIST
        1. Tabulate attempt counts for every candidate endpoint-equivalence group.
        2. Select groups with adequate sample sizes and clear substantive meaning.
        3. Freeze one primary contrast and label all others secondary or exploratory.
        4. For each group, summarize effective moves, excess moves, takebacks,
           resets, checks, first-check distance, omissions, wrong-slot errors, and active time.
        5. Plot distributions or model-based estimates rather than only means.
        6. Use student-level bootstrap or clustered uncertainty for descriptive contrasts.
        7. If fitting a model, specify outcome, link, covariates, student effect,
           item effect, missingness rule, diagnostics, and estimand before fitting.
        8. Select representative trajectories using a reproducible rule such as
           medoids, quantiles, or nearest-to-group-median feature vectors.
        9. State explicitly that observed patterns support behavioral descriptions,
           not direct labels such as misconception, guessing, or reasoning strategy.
        -->
      </subsection>
    </section>